A||||6||Richard Parnell||2.5 Valve cover gasket||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||04-15-2000||08:14 PM||||I'm resealing the valve cover on my 2.5 4 banger.  Once I got it off I saw that it wasn't as described in the manual.  It has an old cork gasket with a metal center.  I did buy a gasket even though the manual says just use a sealer.  My question is this, once I cleaned off the valve cover I noticed that every few inches there is a small raised metal bump or nipple along the area where you would put the sealant and gasket.  What is that?  Do I need to watch out for any sealing problems using the sealer and new cork gasket?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Patrick||04-15-2000||08:41 PM||||<BR>I've never had the valve cover off my 2.5L, but wouldn't those bumps be there just to help hold the gasket in place?<P>I think you would need to be more concerned with the valve cover being bent down a bit where the bolts go through it. If your cover is bent at these locations, you should straighten it out, or even bend it up very slightly at each hole. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||04-15-2000||08:45 PM||||Thanks.  The bolt holes are rather uneven.  Looking closer, those bumps look like someone hit the valve cover from the top with some type of sharp tipped hole starter or metal marker.Anyway got everything cleaned up and am about to re seal the cover.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fotofrank||04-15-2000||08:57 PM||||I look at two valve covers today for 2.5L engins. Both had the "dimple" as you described. Best guess is to tnsur a good ground. (no arc, no spark --- car live longer)||||reg||6
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||04-15-2000||09:24 PM||||The dimples don't go far enough down to ground on anything.  Besides the gasket is between the cover and the block.  How long after reinstalling the valve cover with new sealer should I wait before starting up the engine?||||reg||7
Z||000005||Rodrv6||04-16-2000||12:09 AM||||The dimples are original.  The factory installed the valve covers on the Dukes with RTV type sealer--no gasket.  The dimples are to keep all the sealer from being squeezed out when the valve cover is installed.  Some folks have recommended using a cork type gasket instead of just RTV when reinstalling a valve cover, but I have had good luck with just sealer.  Both seal surfaces must be completly free of any oil traces or the RTV will leak.||||reg||6
Z||000006||theogre||04-16-2000||10:12 PM||||After reading the full text of both the NTSB recall and other GM TSR's.........<P>The sealer only method may have been the original install but it is NOT the recomended repair. I beleive GM required a gasket be used for recall repair.(I don't feel like digging thru all that paper right now.)<P>Rubber gaskets are better and less prone to crushing than cork. These are all I'll use unless I can't get one. I spray/paint both sides with either Super 300 or High Tack sealer from Permatex. ((Balkamp Aviation sealer is also a good choice.))||||reg||1
